WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.147
Module six. Price change requests.

2
00:00:05.333 --> 00:00:08.527
Every product in the catalog has one catalog price.

3
00:00:08.527 --> 00:00:12.849
But vendors are different, and sometimes they need a different price.

4
00:00:12.849 --> 00:00:15.104
This module explains how that works.

5
00:00:15.554 --> 00:00:17.623
Why vendors ask for a different price.

6
00:00:18.073 --> 00:00:19.310
How they request it.

7
00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.107
What the admin sees, and how to decide.

8
00:00:22.557 --> 00:00:26.610
Why an approved price only applies to one vendor and one warehouse.

9
00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:28.404
And the same city rule.

10
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.189
The form itself suggests common reasons.

11
00:00:33.639 --> 00:00:36.583
A promotional offer, which is a short term discount.

12
00:00:37.033 --> 00:00:40.660
A margin update, when the vendor's own cost goes up or down.

13
00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:44.353
A bulk discount, because they can sell cheaper in volume.

14
00:00:44.803 --> 00:00:48.256
Or local competition, to match nearby market rates.

15
00:00:48.256 --> 00:00:52.589
A vendor can also ask for a higher price, but the admin decides.

16
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.259
Here's the whole flow.

17
00:00:56.709 --> 00:01:01.935
While adding or editing an SKU, the vendor enters a requested price and a reason.

18
00:01:02.385 --> 00:01:04.391
The admin gets a notification.

19
00:01:04.841 --> 00:01:09.491
The admin compares the catalog price, the current price and the requested price.

20
00:01:09.941 --> 00:01:11.797
The admin accepts or rejects.

21
00:01:12.247 --> 00:01:15.725
If accepted, the new price goes live, for that vendor only.

22
00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:20.053
Let's watch this in the live demo.

23
00:01:23.867 --> 00:01:29.206
This vendor has two warehouses: abc in Lucknow, which is pending, and Kengeri, which

24
00:01:29.206 --> 00:01:29.968
Is approved.

25
00:01:51.867 --> 00:01:56.811
In Add new SKU, below the policy section, there's a checkbox to allow cash on

26
00:01:56.811 --> 00:01:58.480
Delivery for this listing.

27
00:02:15.867 --> 00:02:21.871
Here's product pricing from the master catalog: MRP nine hundred, selling price six

28
00:02:21.871 --> 00:02:26.790
Hundred sixty one, inclusive of GST. Below it, Request price change.

29
00:02:34.267 --> 00:02:37.661
The vendor requests five hundred fifty, with a reason.

30
00:02:37.661 --> 00:02:41.243
In inventory, the price request status now shows pending.

31
00:02:51.067 --> 00:02:55.931
In the admin panel, the request opens in a window called vendor change requests.

32
00:02:56.381 --> 00:02:59.730
The catalog price, which is the universal master price.

33
00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:03.210
The current price that's active for this vendor right now.

34
00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:06.220
The requested price, proposed by the vendor.

35
00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:07.843
The vendor's reason.

36
00:03:08.293 --> 00:03:13.402
Accept or reject. A note reminds you that accepting changes the price for this

37
00:03:13.402 --> 00:03:16.677
Vendor only, and the catalog price stays the same.

38
00:03:17.127 --> 00:03:20.775
And a second tab, for warranty and return policy requests.

39
00:03:23.167 --> 00:03:25.087
Let's watch this in the live demo.

40
00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:32.502
Back in the vendor's inventory, the request shows pending.

41
00:03:32.502 --> 00:03:35.172
Now we go to admin, Warehouses and Vendors.

42
00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:00.811
A small badge on the vendor's row shows there's a change request.

43
00:04:00.811 --> 00:04:03.940
Clicking it opens the vendor change requests window.

44
00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:19.701
Catalog price six sixty one, current price six sixty one, requested five fifty.

45
00:04:19.701 --> 00:04:22.137
The reason from the vendor: too high.

46
00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:35.679
Accept price is clicked. Below, you can see another request that was rejected, where

47
00:04:35.679 --> 00:04:37.775
A vendor asked for a higher price.

48
00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:50.735
So here's what happened in numbers.

49
00:04:51.185 --> 00:04:54.375
The catalog price was six hundred sixty one rupees.

50
00:04:54.375 --> 00:04:56.753
That stays the same for everyone else.

51
00:04:57.203 --> 00:04:59.528
The vendor requested five hundred fifty.

52
00:04:59.978 --> 00:05:04.807
After approval, five fifty is live, but only for this vendor, and only for that

53
00:05:04.807 --> 00:05:05.418
Warehouse.

54
00:05:07.833 --> 00:05:09.753
Let's watch this in the live demo.

55
00:05:13.567 --> 00:05:17.824
In the vendor's inventory, the status now says accepted, five fifty.

56
00:05:17.824 --> 00:05:22.143
And in the customer app, the product shows five hundred fifty rupees.

57
00:05:46.367 --> 00:05:51.438
This is the rule. An approved price is specific to one vendor, and one warehouse.

58
00:05:51.438 --> 00:05:56.447
Other vendors still see the catalog price. And as you'll see next, even the same

59
00:05:56.447 --> 00:05:59.828
Vendor's other warehouses still use the catalog price.

60
00:06:33.533 --> 00:06:35.453
Let's watch this in the live demo.

61
00:06:39.267 --> 00:06:43.534
Now the vendor adds a second warehouse in Lucknow, and the admin approves it.

62
00:07:24.867 --> 00:07:29.539
The warehouse details are edited: name, address, pin code and timings.

63
00:07:51.267 --> 00:07:55.950
Then the same floor tile is added again as a new SKU, this time in the new

64
00:07:55.950 --> 00:07:58.798
Warehouse, without requesting a price change.

65
00:08:19.267 --> 00:08:21.230
Look at the result in the inventory.

66
00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:26.074
Warehouse one shows five fifty, because the price change was approved there.

67
00:08:26.524 --> 00:08:31.912
Warehouse two shows six sixty one, the catalog price, because no request was made

68
00:08:31.912 --> 00:08:37.232
For it. In the demo, the customer app even showed six sixty one on the card, but

69
00:08:37.232 --> 00:08:40.092
Five fifty inside, which confused everyone.

70
00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:44.420
Let's watch this in the live demo.

71
00:08:48.233 --> 00:08:52.436
Here's the inventory with both rows: the same tile at two prices.

72
00:09:16.233 --> 00:09:21.081
The team discussed this. A vendor might have a main branch in Lucknow and another

73
00:09:21.081 --> 00:09:24.432
Warehouse in Bangalore, and want different prices there.

74
00:09:24.432 --> 00:09:25.150
That's fair.

75
00:09:48.233 --> 00:09:52.913
But two warehouses in the same city with different prices confuses customers.

76
00:09:52.913 --> 00:09:54.676
So they agreed on a new rule.

77
00:10:32.233 --> 00:10:37.279
The same city rule. A vendor cannot set different prices for warehouses in the same

78
00:10:37.279 --> 00:10:42.020
City. Different cities, like Lucknow and Bangalore, can have different prices.

79
00:10:42.020 --> 00:10:46.822
This validation was being built at the time of the session, so until it's live,

80
00:10:46.822 --> 00:10:50.409
Admins should check this manually before accepting a price.

81
00:11:30.667 --> 00:11:32.864
Before you click accept, run through this.

82
00:11:33.314 --> 00:11:34.722
Read the vendor's reason.

83
00:11:35.172 --> 00:11:38.543
Compare it with the catalog price, and with nearby vendors.

84
00:11:38.993 --> 00:11:40.422
Check the same city rule.

85
00:11:40.872 --> 00:11:43.944
Remember, it only applies to this vendor and warehouse.

86
00:11:44.394 --> 00:11:47.850
And reject unclear requests, with a note explaining why.

87
00:11:50.267 --> 00:11:53.659
Quick check. The admin approved five fifty for vendor A.

88
00:11:53.659 --> 00:11:56.688
What price does vendor B see for the same product?

89
00:12:00.638 --> 00:12:05.573
The catalog price. An approved price only applies to the vendor and warehouse that

90
00:12:05.573 --> 00:12:06.356
Requested it.

91
00:12:09.167 --> 00:12:10.682
Let's sum up module six.

92
00:12:11.132 --> 00:12:14.844
Vendors request a price and a reason while adding an SKU.

93
00:12:15.294 --> 00:12:18.899
The admin compares the catalog, current and requested price.

94
00:12:19.349 --> 00:12:22.784
An approved price is only for that vendor and that warehouse.

95
00:12:23.234 --> 00:12:26.626
Same city means same price. Check it manually for now.

96
00:12:27.076 --> 00:12:31.342
And vendors can request higher prices too, but the admin decides.

97
00:12:31.342 --> 00:12:33.049
Next, orders and dispatch.

98
00:12:35.433 --> 00:12:40.297
That's the end of module 6. Next up, module 7: Orders and dispatch.
